  <article>
    <p class="lead">A Catalyst is the person who turns a stalled situation into motion. Something has been stuck for weeks, everyone agrees it matters, and nothing moves, until you walk in, create a sense of urgency, and get people actually doing it. <strong>Your gift is not the plan or the product; it is the spark that makes a group move and keep moving.</strong> In the Best Answer Hub Work Signature, this is the People Catalyst: the strength of initiative, momentum, and mobilizing others to act.</p>

    <p>This guide is part of the Best Answer Hub map of the <a href="/playbooks/career-strengths-types/">eight Work Signature types</a>. It covers what the Catalyst strength really is, how to tell if it is one of yours, the modern careers that pay for it, why momentum is measurably valuable, how it holds up as AI automates the mechanics of getting things done, and the blind spot that comes with being the one who always starts.</p>

    <div class="cav">Start here</div>
    <h2 id="what">What is the Catalyst strength?</h2>
    <p>The Catalyst strength is the ability to get stalled or unstarted work moving and rally people to act on it. Best Answer Hub names this type the People Catalyst because the defining move is social: not doing the work alone, but generating the urgency and energy that gets a group off the fence. The real test is durable movement, not activity. A Catalyst is not the person who is simply busy; it is the one whose push leaves something running after they step back.</p>
    <p>That distinction matters because momentum is where most efforts fail. In his study of more than a hundred companies attempting major change, John Kotter found that well over half stumbled in the very first phase, the one about establishing urgency, because they never built enough energy to overcome the pull of business as usual (<a href="https://hbr.org/1995/05/leading-change-why-transformation-efforts-fail-2" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kotter, Harvard Business Review, 1995</a>). The Catalyst is the person who clears that first hurdle.</p>

    <div class="cav">Sound familiar?</div>
    <h2 id="spot">How do you know if Catalyst is one of your strengths?</h2>
    <p>You can usually spot the Catalyst strength by what happens around you: things that were stuck start moving once you get involved. Best Answer Hub sees it show up as a set of consistent tells, and if most describe you, Catalyst is probably near the top of your Work Signature.</p>
    <div class="sec">
      <ul class="pillars">
        <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>You get stuck things unstuck.</b> A project that has drifted for weeks starts moving after you step in.</div></li>
        <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>You create urgency.</b> You can make a group feel that now, not someday, is when this happens.</div></li>
        <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>You rally people.</b> You are the one who gets others to commit and actually act, not just agree.</div></li>
        <li><span class="dot">4</span><div><b>You start easily.</b> Beginning is not the hard part for you; you would rather launch than wait for perfect conditions.</div></li>
        <li><span class="dot">5</span><div><b>Stalled energy frustrates you.</b> A team that keeps meeting without moving drives you to push it forward.</div></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">Momentum is not the same as busyness</div><p>A Catalyst creates movement that lasts, not motion for its own sake. The honest test is whether the thing you started is still running after you step away. Getting people busy is easy; getting them to move on something that sticks is the actual strength. Best Answer Hub measures the difference.</p></div>

    <div class="cav">Where it pays</div>
    <h2 id="careers">Which careers fit a Catalyst?</h2>
    <p>Catalyst strengths pay best where the job is to get people to move: to adopt, to buy, to give, to change. Best Answer Hub matches catalysts to current roles built around mobilizing others rather than working alone, and several combine solid growth with strong pay.</p>
