    <p>Two of the most catalyst-shaped roles, chief of staff and founder, have no standalone government category, so treat them qualitatively rather than by a wage figure. What the whole list shares is a job description that reduces to one thing: get other people to move. Best Answer Hub tags each match with an AI outlook, and the mobilizing roles skew human-led, because rallying a team is not something a system does for you.</p>

    <div class="cav">The evidence</div>
    <h2 id="value">Why does momentum pay off?</h2>
    <p>Because progress itself is the strongest fuel for motivation, and a Catalyst manufactures progress. In a landmark study tracking 238 professionals across 26 teams through nearly 12,000 daily diary entries, Teresa Amabile and Steven Kramer found that of everything that lifts people during a workday, the single most important is making progress in meaningful work. Steps forward showed up on 76% of people's best days, against just 25% of their worst (<a href="https://hbr.org/2011/05/the-power-of-small-wins" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Amabile and Kramer, Harvard Business Review, 2011</a>).</p>
