      </svg>
      <p class="chart-src">Source: Amabile and Kramer, "The Power of Small Wins," Harvard Business Review, 2011 (238 professionals, 26 teams, about 12,000 diary entries).</p>
    </figure>
    <p>That is the Catalyst's economic case, measured. A team that is moving is a team that is motivated, and the person who starts and sustains the movement is doing something the research says matters more than almost anything else. It is why Best Answer Hub treats the ability to create momentum as a genuine strength, not just enthusiasm.</p>

    <div class="cav">The honest bit</div>
    <h2 id="ai">Does AI threaten people whose strength is catalyzing?</h2>
    <p>AI takes over the mechanics of momentum but not the human spark that creates it, which makes the Catalyst strength more valuable, not less. Software is genuinely good at the administration of getting things done: reminders, status tracking, nudges, and surfacing what has stalled. What it cannot do is make a reluctant person care, or generate real urgency in a room. That is social and emotional work, and the evidence says demand for it is climbing.</p>
    <p>McKinsey's analysis of the skill shift under automation projected that demand for social and emotional skills would rise 26% in the United States by 2030, and that the single fastest-growing skill in that category, entrepreneurship and initiative taking, would jump 33%, calling these the skills machines are a long way from mastering (<a href="https://www.mckinsey.com/featured-insights/future-of-work/skill-shift-automation-and-the-future-of-the-workforce" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">McKinsey, 2018</a>). Initiative is the Catalyst's core, and it sits at the top of the list of what stays human. The World Economic Forum agrees, ranking leadership and social influence among the core skills rising fastest in importance for 2025 (<a href="https://www.weforum.org/publications/the-future-of-jobs-report-2025/in-full/3-skills-outlook/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WEF, 2025</a>).</p>

    <div class="cav">The trap</div>
    <h2 id="shadow">What is the shadow side of the Catalyst strength?</h2>
    <p>The Catalyst's blind spot is starting more than gets finished, and mistaking motion for progress. The same energy that gets things moving can pile initiative on initiative until nothing lands, and can confuse being busy with getting somewhere. In a study of managers, Heike Bruch and Sumantra Ghoshal found that only about 10% spent their time in a purposeful, focused way, while a large group ran on high energy and low focus, feeling a desperate need to do something, anything (<a href="https://hbr.org/2002/02/beware-the-busy-manager" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bruch and Ghoshal, Harvard Business Review, 2002</a>). That is the Catalyst running unchecked.</p>
    <p>There is a human cost too. When urgency becomes constant and initiatives keep stacking, people stop following. Gartner research reported that employees' willingness to support workplace change collapsed from 74% in 2016 to just 43% in 2022, as the number of changes they faced rose roughly fivefold (<a href="https://hbr.org/2023/05/employees-are-losing-patience-with-change-initiatives" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gartner via Harvard Business Review, 2023</a>). Best Answer Hub names this shadow so the Catalyst strength does not burn out the very people it depends on. Where a Navigator plans and never mobilizes, and an Investigator analyzes and never acts, the Catalyst mobilizes and never finishes.</p>
    <div class="callout"><div class="h">How Catalysts cover the blind spot</div><p>Two habits help most: cap the number of things you start at once, and treat finishing as the win rather than launching. Before you rally people to a new push, ask whether the last one actually landed and is still running. Pair with someone whose strength is follow-through, so the movement you create is carried across the line rather than abandoned for the next spark.</p></div>

    <div class="cav">Prove it</div>
    <h2 id="resume">How do you show Catalyst strength on a resume and in interviews?</h2>
    <p>For a Catalyst, the evidence is movement that stuck, so frame each line around what you restarted, who you rallied, and what kept running afterward. Best Answer Hub advises naming the stalled state before you, the specific people you moved, and the outcome that persisted, because the honest proof of a catalyst is durable movement, not a launch that fizzled. A line that says you restarted a migration stalled for eight months and it was still adopted two quarters later beats any list of things you kicked off.</p>
    <p>This kind of initiative is measurably tied to career success, but only when it produces real results. A meta-analysis covering more than a hundred thousand employees found that a proactive personality predicts higher pay, more promotions, and greater career satisfaction, and that the effect runs through actual performance and contribution, not proactivity for its own sake (<a href="https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2022.979412/full" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Peng et al., Frontiers in Psychology, 2022</a>). In interviews, tell the story of the thing that kept moving after you let go.</p>
    <blockquote>A Catalyst is measured by what keeps moving. <span class="accent">Show the thing that was still running after you stepped away.</span></blockquote>

    <div class="cav">Better together</div>
    <h2 id="pair">Which strengths pair well with Catalyst?</h2>
    <p>Catalyst is strongest next to a strength that carries the movement across the line. A Catalyst paired with Anchor is the ideal combination: one creates the momentum, the other provides the follow-through that turns it into something that lasts, directly covering the started-not-finished blind spot. A Catalyst paired with Navigator gets direction under the energy, so the push points at the right goal. Because Best Answer Hub ranks all eight strengths, you can see which pairing your own Work Signature already holds and which you may need from a teammate.</p>

